# 4. CORE PRODUCT PRINCIPLES

The application must follow these principles.

## 4.1 Digital identity

Every resource has a unique, permanent identifier.

Changing the resource name, owner, custodian, or location must not change its identity.

## 4.2 Safety before convenience

Booking eligibility must be checked by trusted backend logic.

The frontend may display the result but must not be able to override it.

## 4.3 Explainable decisions

Every failed eligibility check must have:

* A stable reason code.
* A readable explanation.
* The affected requirement.
* A corrective action where appropriate.
* The applicable policy version.
* An evaluation timestamp.

## 4.4 Complete lifecycle

A resource passport must retain relevant history from registration through operational use, maintenance, custody changes, restrictions, and retirement.

## 4.5 Least privilege

Users must only access the data and operations required for their assigned responsibilities.

## 4.6 Auditability

Important changes must be attributable to an authenticated user or identified system process.

## 4.7 No silent failure

Errors must be visible and actionable.

Never show a success message when the database operation failed.

## 4.8 Progressive implementation

Build the application in working stages.

Do not create every page first and postpone the real backend until the end.

